<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="weave-discovery" title="How a weave is found">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        Nothing scans a classpath for <code>@Weave</code>. Every module that declares one ships a
        manifest, written while it compiled. A driver reads the manifests it can see. It parses
        each class they name and hands the planner what survives.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["The class you annotated"] --> B["aether-weaver-processor writes META-INF/aether/weaves.json"]
    B --> C["A driver reads whichever manifests it sees"]
    C --> D["One candidate per class named"]
    D --> E["The parser reads that class file"]
    E --> F{"Configuration keeps it?"}
    F -->|no| G["Dropped, after parsing"]
    F -->|yes| H["Handed to the planner"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="The manifest" id="manifest">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="Written by the processor, once">
                <p>
                    <code>aether-weaver-processor</code> collects what a compilation declared. It
                    writes the manifest in the final round, into the compilation output at
                    <code>META-INF/aether/weaves.json</code>. A weave the processor refuses is
                    recorded too. A refused extension is not: only the holder's registration
                    survives. <a href="install.topic">Install</a> has the pom that puts the
                    processor on the annotation processor path.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Absent where nothing was declared">
                <p>
                    A module declaring neither a weave nor an
                    <a href="extension-methods.topic">extension</a> gets no file at all. That is
                    the common case: most modules in a build declare nothing. A missing manifest
                    can also mean the processor was never configured. A runtime driver that finds
                    none anywhere warns with <code>AW2302</code>.
                    <a href="install.topic">Install</a> has the remedy.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Merged over what is already there">
                <p>
                    Writing merges by class name over the file already in the output directory. An
                    incremental build therefore keeps the entries it did not recompile. A document
                    from a newer schema is refused whole, as <code>AW2301</code>. Upgrade Aether
                    Weaver, or rebuild that artefact against this version.
                    <a href="manifest-format.topic">The manifest reference</a> has the keys.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where each driver looks" id="drivers">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Driver</td>
                <td>Its weaves come from</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The Maven plugin</td>
                <td>the module's own output</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The Java agent</td>
                <td>every manifest it can reach</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A weaving class loader, from <code>create</code></td>
                <td>its roots, and its parent</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A weaving class loader, from the constructor</td>
                <td>nothing — the <code>Weaver</code> you already built</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The testkit</td>
                <td>the classes you name</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            Under Maven the classpath is still read, for
            <a href="extension-methods.topic">extensions</a> and for one warning.
            <code>AW3010</code> names an artefact that declares a weave and that nothing here asked
            for. Declare that dependency directly, or exclude it.
        </p>

        <p>
            Under <code>weave-tests</code>, though, the check reaches too far. It compares only
            against the test output directory, not the module's own main classes. A weave declared
            there is reported too. Neither remedy applies: it is not a dependency at all.
            <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a> covers what each driver reaches and
            what each one costs.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <procedure title="From an entry to a parsed weave" id="steps">
        <step>
            <p>
                Each entry becomes a candidate: a class name, and the artefact its manifest came
                from. Nothing else in the entry is used.
            </p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>
                The bytes are read out of that same artefact, never out of the classpath at large.
                A class it no longer holds is <code>AW2300</code> under a runtime driver. Rebuild
                the artefact, whose manifest has outlived the class. The Maven goal skips it
                without a word.
            </p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>
                The parser reads the class file. Targets, handlers and members all come from there;
                <a href="weaving-model.topic">the weaving model</a> lists them. A class carrying no
                <code>@Weave</code> is dropped in silence. Anything else the parser refuses is
                reported first.
            </p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>
                Configuration decides last, and only under a runtime driver. The order matters. A
                weave switched off is still parsed, so its faults surface now, not on the
                deployment that switches it back on.
            </p>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <chapter title="Kept, or dropped" id="kept">
        <p>
            Two settings judge a parsed weave. Neither reaches the
            <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Maven plugin or the testkit</a>.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>An <code>enabled</code> override on the weave's binary name settles it, whatever
                tags say.</p></li>
            <li><p>Otherwise the tag filter answers. One excluded tag rejects the weave. Include
                anything by name, and a weave carrying no tag is rejected too.
                <a href="configuration-keys.topic">The configuration reference</a> has both keys.</p></li>
        </list>

        <note>
            <p>
                A dropped weave is gone before the plan exists. It reaches no gate on
                <a href="pipeline.topic">the weaving pipeline</a>, and changes no fingerprint.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="One class name, two artefacts" id="duplicates">
        <p>
            A runtime driver reads many manifests. Two of them may name one weave class. That is
            <code>AW2303</code>, a warning. Remove the duplicate dependency, or rename one of the
            weaves. Both candidates are kept, each read from its own artefact.
        </p>

        <p>
            Which class of that name loads is the classpath's decision. The tie they leave in the
            plan falls back to discovery order. With dissolving weaves that order reaches the
            fingerprint too. <a href="ordering.topic">Ordering</a> has the key it ties on, and
            <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> what a moved fingerprint costs.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="A source of your own" id="sources">
        <p>
            <code>WeaveSource</code> is the interface behind the manifest reader. Nothing looks one
            up: no service file is read for it. A <a href="plugins.topic">weaver plugin</a>
            registers injectors and selectors, never a source. Your own source is one your code
            constructs and calls itself, feeding the same parse.
        </p>
    </chapter>

